<article class="env-lemma" id="06IH">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06IH">Lemma <span data-tag="06IH">90.12.2</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{F}$ be a predeformation category such that $\overline{\mathcal{F}}$ satisfies (S2)<a class="footnotemark" href="#a0001605264" id="a0001605264-mark"><sup>1</sup></a>. Then $T \mathcal{F}$ has a natural $k$-vector space structure. For any finite dimensional vector space $V$ we have $\overline{\mathcal{F}}(k[V]) = T\mathcal{F} \otimes _ k V$ functorially in $V$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let us write $F = \overline{\mathcal{F}} : \mathcal{C}_\Lambda \to \textit{Sets}$. This is a predeformation functor and $F$ satisfies (S2). By Lemma <a href="/tag/06HZ" data-tag="06HZ">90.10.4</a> (and the translation of Remark <a href="/tag/06HY" data-tag="06HY">90.10.3</a>) we see that 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  F(A \times _ k k[V]) \longrightarrow F(A) \times F(k[V])  \]
</div>
    <p>
       is a bijection for every finite dimensional vector space $V$ and every $A \in \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C}_\Lambda )$. In particular, if $A = k[W]$ then we see that $F(k[W] \times _ k k[V]) = F(k[W]) \times F(k[V])$. In other words, the hypotheses of Lemma <a href="/tag/06IA" data-tag="06IA">90.11.8</a> hold and we see that $TF = T \mathcal{F}$ has a natural $k$-vector space structure. The final assertion follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/06IE" data-tag="06IE">90.11.15</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>